    "sources": {
      "decaf": [
        "Direction: COARSER (not finer). Older 'finer for decaf' folk-wisdom assumed weakened cell walls → faster extraction, but post-2019 research points the other way.",
        "Mechanism: decaffeination embrittles the bean → grinding produces more fines → fines accumulate as a low-permeability layer in the bed → percolation slows and channels → coarsen to restore flow.",
        "Sources: Jonathan Gagné, 'Grind Size, Fines, and Permeability' (Coffee ad Astra, 2020); Mark Al-Shemmeri Medium articles on decaf brittleness; Scientific Reports 2024 paper on fines distribution and extraction; Food Chemistry 2024 study on decaf compound retention.",
        "Magnitude: 0–1 step coarser, brew-family dependent. Percolation (PULSAR / V60 / MOKA / ESPRESSO) +1; immersion (FRENCH_PRESS / AEROPRESS / COLD_BREW) +0 (fines impact minimal when whole bed steeps).",
        "Roast modifier: dark / espresso / medium-dark roasts +1 more step coarser (already brittle from caramelization, decaf compounds the effect).",
        "Process relief: Swiss Water, Mountain Water, and CO₂ supercritical processes preserve bean structure better → -1 step relief (less coarsening needed). Solvent processes (EA Sugarcane, EA Direct, MC Direct) get no relief — more fines expected.",
        "Final steps clamped to ≥ 0; recommendation moves toward rangeEnd, never beyond."
      ],
      "chemex-official-anchor": [
        "Baratza Encore ESP Operations Manual January 2023 v1.0 p.5: Chemex starting setting 30; https://assets.breville.com/ZCG495/manual-encoreesp-v1-0-en-010923.pdf; verified 2026-09-30",
        "Setting 30 is a published starting point, not a published optimal range. Other grinders use generic medium-coarse guidance until method-specific evidence is verified."
      ]
    }
  },
